# DNS (Domain Name System) – Complete Interview Notes

# 1. What is DNS?

DNS (Domain Name System) is the Internet's "phone book."

Humans remember domain names like:

```
google.com
amazon.com
github.com
```

Computers communicate using IP addresses like:

```
142.250.183.14
140.82.114.4
```

DNS translates a **domain name** into an **IP address** so the browser knows which server to connect to.

---

# 2. Interview Definition

> DNS (Domain Name System) is a distributed and hierarchical naming system that converts human-readable domain names into IP addresses, allowing clients to locate and communicate with servers over the Internet.

---

# 3. Why Do We Need DNS?

Without DNS, users would have to remember IP addresses instead of domain names.

Example:

Instead of typing:

```
https://google.com
```

you would have to type:

```
https://142.250.183.14
```

DNS makes the Internet easy to use.

---

# 4. Real-Life Analogy

Think of your phone contacts.

You save:

```
Mom
Dad
John
```

instead of remembering every phone number.

When you tap "Mom," your phone looks up the stored number and makes the call.

DNS works the same way:

```
google.com
```

↓

```
142.250.183.14
```

---

# 5. Where Does DNS Fit?

```
Browser

↓

DNS Lookup

↓

IP Address

↓

TCP Connection

↓

HTTPS (TLS)

↓

HTTP Request

↓

Server
```

DNS is the first networking step before any HTTP request is sent.

