# 6. Complete DNS Resolution Process

Suppose the user enters:

```
https://www.example.com
```

The browser performs the following steps:

```
User

↓

Browser

↓

Browser Cache

↓

Operating System Cache

↓

Router Cache (optional)

↓

ISP DNS Resolver

↓

Root DNS Server

↓

TLD DNS Server

↓

Authoritative DNS Server

↓

IP Address Returned

↓

Browser Connects to Server
```

---

# 7. Browser Cache

The browser first checks whether it already knows the IP address.

If found:

```
Browser Cache

↓

IP Address Found

↓

Skip DNS Lookup
```

This makes websites load faster.

---

# 8. Operating System Cache

If the browser cache misses, the operating system checks its DNS cache.

Example:

Windows

```
ipconfig /displaydns
```

Linux/macOS

```
systemd-resolve --statistics
```

or

```
dig
```

---

# 9. Recursive Resolver (ISP DNS Server)

If the OS doesn't know the IP, it asks a recursive DNS resolver.

Examples:

* ISP DNS
* Google DNS (8.8.8.8)
* Cloudflare DNS (1.1.1.1)

The resolver performs the lookup on behalf of the client.

---

# 10. Root DNS Servers

The resolver first contacts a Root DNS Server.

The root server does **not** know the IP address.

Instead, it replies:

> "Ask the .com DNS servers."

```
google.com

↓

Root Server

↓

Go to .com servers
```

---

# 11. Top-Level Domain (TLD) Server

The resolver then contacts the TLD server.

Examples:

```
.com
.org
.net
.edu
.in
.io
```

The TLD server replies:

> "Ask Google's authoritative DNS server."

---

# 12. Authoritative DNS Server

This server stores the actual DNS records.

Example:

```
google.com

↓

142.250.183.14
```

It sends the IP address back to the resolver.

---

# 13. DNS Records

DNS servers store different types of records.

## A Record

Maps a domain to an IPv4 address.

```
google.com

↓

142.250.183.14
```

---

## AAAA Record

Maps a domain to an IPv6 address.

---

## CNAME Record

Creates an alias.

Example:

```
www.example.com

↓

example.com
```

---

## MX Record

Specifies mail servers.

Used for email delivery.

Example:

```
gmail.com

↓

Google Mail Servers
```

---

## NS Record

Specifies which DNS servers are authoritative for the domain.

---

## TXT Record

Stores text information.

Commonly used for:

* Domain verification
* SPF
* DKIM
* DMARC

---

# 14. What is TTL?

TTL stands for **Time To Live**.

It tells DNS resolvers how long they may cache a DNS record.

Example:

```
TTL = 3600 seconds
```

The IP address can be reused for one hour before another lookup is required.

Higher TTL:

* Faster lookups
* Fewer DNS queries

Lower TTL:

* Easier to change IP addresses
* More DNS traffic

---

# 15. Recursive vs Iterative Queries

## Recursive Query

The client asks one resolver.

The resolver does all the work.

```
Browser

↓

DNS Resolver

↓

Root

↓

TLD

↓

Authoritative

↓

Browser
```

Most browsers use recursive queries.

---

## Iterative Query

Each DNS server tells the client where to ask next.

The client performs multiple lookups.

Less common for browsers.

